{%- if lcp -%}
<a class="u-url sr-only" href="{{ page.url }}">{{ title }}</a>{% comment %} Visual fix: sr-only so the permalink's own title isn't printed twice (h1 above already carries p-name) — see types/article.liquid's comment. {% endcomment %}
{%- if date %}<time class="dt-published" datetime="{{ date | isoInstant }}">{{ date | humanDate }}</time>{% endif %}
{%- if bookmarkOf %}{% include "partials/link-preview.liquid", linkUrl: bookmarkOf, linkClass: "u-bookmark-of", linkText: title %}{% endif %}
{%- comment %} Task 18 (§7.4): see types/article.liquid for why h2,h3. {% endcomment -%}
<div class="content-grid e-content"><heading-anchors selector="h2,h3">{{ content }}</heading-anchors></div>
{%- else -%}
{%- comment %} Task S4 (audit §2.2, corrected — see task-s4-report.md): the
feed-listing branch. Unlike note/like/reply/photo, a bookmark's title is
always meaningful here even when it has none of its own — eleventyComputed.js
gives an untitled bookmark its own fallback (the bookmarkOf URL itself), not
the generic "<Type> · <date>" label the other titleless types get, so it
never duplicates the <time> next to it. One shape covers both: the source's
link-list, no body. `.posts .date` no longer hides <time> in this port (see
sass/theme-posts.scss) — <time> now sits after `.tags` so the dotted leader
runs from the title to the date, same order as the other titleless types.
{% endcomment -%}
<a class="posts-link p-name u-url" href="{{ page.url }}">{{ title }}</a>
<span class="tags"></span>
{%- if date %}<time class="date dt-published" datetime="{{ date | isoInstant }}">{{ date | humanDate }}</time>{% endif %}
{%- endif -%}
